{"text": "IN THE DISTRICT COURT OF JOHNSON COUNTY\nSTATE OF NEBRASKA\n\nCHARLES W. HERBSTER, Case No. CI 22-27\nPlaintiff/Counterclaim\nDefendant,\nOBJECTION TO PLAIN-\nv. TIFF/COUNTERCLAIM-DE-\nFENDANT\u2019S MOTION TO\nJULIE SLAMA, QUASH AND FOR PRO-\nTECT DE\nDefendant/Counter- CTIVE ORDER\n\nclaim Plaintiff.\n\nCOMES NOW Defendant Julie Slama (\u201cSen. Slama\u201d), by and\nthrough her counsel of record, and objects to Charles Herbster\u2019s\n(\u201cHerbster\u201d) Motion to Quash and for Protective Order. In support of\nher objection, Sen. Slama states as follows:\n\n1. Sen. Slama noticed the deposition of Charles Herbster on\nApril 25, 2022, in full compliance with the Nebraska Rules and all pro-\ncedural requirements. See Neb. Ct. R. Disc. \u00a7 6-330.\n2. On that same date, Sen. Slama\u2019s counsel served the notice\nby email and First-Class U.S. Mail on Herbster\u2019s counsel of record. See\nM. Jensen Aff. at {/{] 3-4, Exhibits 1-2. Herbster\u2019s counsel acknowledged\nreceipt of the notice on April 26, 2022. Id. at J 5-6, Exhibits 3-4.\n3. Notwithstanding Herbster\u2019s dilatory, eleventh-hour fil-\nings\u2014which follow nearly two full business weeks during which he and\nhis attorneys have had notice of the May 6th deposition\u2014Herbster re-\nmains under a legal obligation to appear and give testimony on that\ndate. However, in light of his stated refusal to appear, sufficient grounds\nfor sanctions now already exist for his failure to appear and cooperate\nin properly served discovery in a lawsuit he initiated during the heat of\na political campaign he now wields as an excuse for his purported una-\nvailability.",
